{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DHLMP6HBajh11mvuZG6HT7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JMQPp2abCMpPjeZtsiQmjD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\n\\title{When Psychometric Contamination Detection Meets Heterogeneous Model Populations:\\\\A Negative-Result Study on DIF-Based Benchmark Monitoring}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nBenchmark contamination threatens the validity of LLM evaluation, yet existing detection methods degrade under reinforcement learning (RL) training.\nWe investigate whether Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric technique---can serve as an RL-robust contamination signal by operating on group-level response patterns rather than within-model output distributions.\nWe test this hypothesis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and cross-validate on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth.\nOur findings are predominantly negative.\nThe unidimensional IRT model required for DIF severely misfits the heterogeneous model population (RMSEA\\,=\\,0.11, CFI\\,=\\,0.78), and architecture differences (encoder-only vs.\\ decoder-only vs.\\ MoE) generate massive spurious DIF that inflates the false-positive rate to 0.12.\nThe claimed RL-robustness advantage is not observed: DIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54 under RL cohorts, comparable to or worse than traditional detectors (ConStat AUROC\\,=\\,0.61).\nA three-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat + DVD) fails to provide complementary coverage due to correlated failure modes ($r$\\,=\\,0.54--0.61).\nBifactor IRT equating only partially converges (73.5\\% of items) and equated rankings reach $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.85, far below psychometric equivalence standards ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$).\nWe characterize the failure modes, identify the practical bounds of item replacement (at most 7\\% of items can be safely replaced), and argue that DIF-based benchmark monitoring requires architecture-stratified modeling before it can be viable.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM benchmarks such as MMLU implicitly assume temporal score comparability: when we say ``Model B outperforms Model A,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent ability rather than differential exposure to test content.\nBenchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption.\nThe problem is compounded by the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1), as Wang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) demonstrate 14--20pp AUROC degradation for reference-free detectors after approximately 156 GRPO steps.\n\nWe hypothesized that Differential Item Functioning (DIF)---a technique from educational testing that detects items behaving differently across examinee groups after controlling for ability---could provide an RL-robust contamination signal.\nThe theoretical argument is that DIF operates on \\emph{group-level} Item Characteristic Curves (ICCs) conditional on ability, making it orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing caused by RL.\nWe further proposed a full pipeline: DIF-based detection, multi-signal ensemble fusion for full-spectrum coverage, and bifactor IRT equating for calibrated item replacement.\n\nThis paper reports the results of this investigation across 10 experiments.\nThe core finding is negative: \\textbf{the DIF-based contamination detection hypothesis does not hold on real data} because the 5,000+ heterogeneous model population massively violates the IRT unidimensionality assumption that DIF requires.\nArchitecture heterogeneity creates spurious DIF that overwhelms the contamination signal.\n\nDespite the negative headline result, we make several empirical contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item We provide the first large-scale empirical characterization of IRT model fit on heterogeneous LLM populations, documenting systematic failure modes.\n    \\item We establish practical bounds on benchmark item replacement under imperfect calibration (at most 7\\% safe replacement).\n    \\item We demonstrate that three contamination detection signals (DIF, ConStat, DVD) exhibit correlated failure modes rather than complementary coverage, challenging the ensemble assumption.\n    \\item We show that bifactor IRT reveals MMLU to be highly multidimensional (general factor explains only 35\\% of variance), with implications for benchmark design.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMin-K\\% Prob identifies contamination through low-perplexity tokens.\nConStat provides FDR-controlled detection via perturbation sensitivity.\nDVD detects variant contamination through generation variance.\nAll these methods operate on within-model output distributions and are vulnerable to RL smoothing.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}\nDIF analysis identifies test items that function differently across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability.\nThe ETS classification system uses both statistical significance and effect-size thresholds ($\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$) to flag items.\nDIF has been applied extensively in human testing but, to our knowledge, never to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}\nGrowing Pains (2026) applies multidimensional IRT to LLM evaluation for extensible benchmark design.\nOur work differs in targeting contamination detection rather than score comparability, though our bifactor analysis yields related insights about benchmark dimensionality.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark regeneration.}\nLiveBench updates monthly from recent sources without psychometric calibration.\nStudent simulation (arXiv 2601.09953) uses capability-ablated LLMs for difficulty estimation.\nNo prior work integrates detection, equating, and regeneration into a closed loop.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\nWe partition models into temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024, or annual/sliding-window cohorts) and fit a unidimensional 2PL IRT model to the binary response matrix.\nFor each item $j$, we compute the Mantel--Haenszel DIF statistic $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},j}$ between the reference (earlier) and focal (later) cohorts.\nItems are classified using the ETS C criterion ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant at $\\alpha = 0.05$).\nA contaminated item should exhibit DIF because exposed models perform better than their overall ability predicts.\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\nWe combine three contamination signals:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF} (ETS C): group-level ICC shift, hypothesized RL-robust.\n    \\item \\textbf{ConStat}: perturbation sensitivity---contaminated models answer originals correctly but perturbations incorrectly.\n    \\item \\textbf{DVD}: generation variance---contaminated items elicit bimodal response distributions.\n\\end{itemize}\nFusion strategies include majority voting (2-of-3) and weighted fusion with weights learned on a validation split.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating and Regeneration}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model with one general factor and subject-specific factors (57 MMLU subjects).\nAnchor items (stable across cohorts, no DIF) enable score equating.\nFlagged items are replaced by LLM-generated alternatives calibrated via student simulation.\nA graceful-degradation framework quantifies validity loss under progressive item replacement.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) unless otherwise noted.\nWe use MMLU-CF as partial ground truth for MMLU and GSM1K for GSM8K.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 1: DIF Detection on Real Data (MMLU)}\n\\label{sec:exp1}\n\nWe apply temporal cohort DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix using three cohort definitions: binary (pre/post-2024), annual, and 6-month sliding window.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_performance} reports detection performance.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection performance on METABENCH MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:dif_performance}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Pre/Post-2024 & Annual & Sliding 6mo \\\\\n\\midrule\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.38 & 0.31 & 0.28 \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & 0.18 & 0.14 & 0.12 \\\\\nFPR & 0.12 & 0.14 & 0.15 \\\\\nF1 ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.33 & 0.28 & 0.25 \\\\\nItems flagged & 1,840 & 2,100 & 2,350 \\\\\nMMLU-CF $\\phi$ correlation & 0.09 & 0.07 & 0.06 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe IRT model severely misfits the data.\nTable~\\ref{tab:irt_fit} shows that all fit indices fall outside acceptable thresholds.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IRT model fit diagnostics.}\n\\label{tab:irt_fit}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nFit Index & Value & Threshold & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.11 & $\\leq 0.06$ & FAIL \\\\\nCFI & 0.78 & $\\geq 0.90$ & FAIL \\\\\nTLI & 0.74 & $\\geq 0.90$ & FAIL \\\\\nSRMR & 0.09 & $\\leq 0.08$ & FAIL \\\\\n$M^2$ $p$-value & $<$0.001 & $>$0.05 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe unidimensionality assumption is rejected ($p < 0.001$).\nItem discrimination variance is 2.8, far exceeding typical values ($<1.0$), indicating that architecture differences---not contamination---dominate the response patterns.\nThe FPR of 0.12 means approximately 1,500 false positives among the 12,508 items, overwhelming any true contamination signal.\nCorrelation with MMLU-CF ground truth is near zero ($\\phi = 0.09$).\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 2: RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:exp2}\n\nWe compare DIF and traditional detectors on SFT-only vs.\\ RL-inclusive cohorts.\nOnly 12 RL-trained models are identifiable in METABENCH.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{RL-robustness comparison: DIF vs.\\ traditional detectors.}\n\\label{tab:rl_comparison}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Retention & $\\Delta$AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS C) & 0.67 & 0.54 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.74 & 0.58 & 0.78 & $-$0.16 \\\\\nConStat & 0.71 & 0.61 & 0.86 & $-$0.10 \\\\\nDVD & 0.68 & 0.55 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\nPerplexity & 0.69 & 0.56 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF TPR under SFT-only vs.\\ RL-inclusive cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:dif_tpr_rl}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size & TPR (SFT) & TPR (RL) & Retention \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.6$ & 0.48 & 0.35 & 0.73 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.42 & 0.29 & 0.69 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.0$ & 0.20 & 0.13 & 0.65 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.6$ & 0.09 & 0.06 & 0.67 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF does not exhibit the hypothesized RL-robustness.\nUnder RL cohorts, DIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54, which is \\emph{worse} than ConStat (0.61).\nThe AUROC gap between DIF and the best traditional method under RL is only 0.02 in DIF's \\emph{disfavor}.\nPower retention ratio is 0.69, comparable to traditional methods (0.78--0.86).\nThe theoretical argument that ``DIF operates on ICC shape, which RL does not alter'' fails because RL training (GRPO/PPO) changes ICC shape itself, not just difficulty.\nAdditionally, the RL cohort contains only 12 models, limiting statistical reliability.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 3: ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp3}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing independently on MMLU with 220 generated perturbation items (82\\% semantic equivalence rate, mean similarity 0.91).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection by effect size and model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size & TPR (SFT) & TPR (RL) & TPR (Mixed) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.55 & 0.18 & 0.38 & 0.06 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.0$ & 0.45 & 0.14 & 0.30 & 0.06 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.6$ & 0.32 & 0.11 & 0.22 & 0.07 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.4$ & 0.19 & 0.08 & 0.14 & 0.07 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat shows moderate power on SFT models for large effects (TPR\\,=\\,0.55 at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$) but degrades sharply under RL (TPR\\,=\\,0.18).\nFDR-controlled power is only 0.28.\nReal-world contamination is predominantly partial memorization; models that only remember answer patterns rather than exact wording are insensitive to perturbations.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 4: DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp4}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 500 sampled items across 13 models with 50 generations per item (65 GPU hours).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nType & TPR & FPR & AUROC & $N$ items \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 0.48 & 0.09 & 0.72 & 120 \\\\\nVariant & 0.22 & 0.09 & 0.58 & 180 \\\\\nPartial & 0.12 & 0.09 & 0.52 & 200 \\\\\nOverall & 0.25 & 0.09 & 0.61 & 500 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD's bimodal-variance assumption breaks down for partial contamination (TPR\\,=\\,0.12), which is the dominant real-world contamination mode.\nThe bimodal distribution rate is only 18\\% for contaminated items vs.\\ 7\\% for clean items---statistically significant but with an effect size too small for reliable per-item detection.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 5: Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:exp5}\n\nWe combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual signal performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR$_{\\geq 1.4}$ & TPR$_{\\geq 1.0}$ & TPR$_{\\geq 0.6}$ & FPR & F1 \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.38 & 0.18 & 0.09 & 0.12 & 0.25 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.38 & 0.30 & 0.22 & 0.06 & 0.32 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.25 & 0.15 & 0.10 & 0.09 & 0.18 \\\\\nMajority (2/3) & 0.38 & 0.19 & 0.11 & 0.05 & 0.30 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & 0.52 & 0.30 & 0.18 & 0.11 & 0.35 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Signal correlation matrix.}\n\\label{tab:correlation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nSignal Pair & Pearson $r$ & Flagged-Item Overlap \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF--ConStat & 0.61 & 68\\% \\\\\nDIF--DVD & 0.54 & 59\\% \\\\\nConStat--DVD & 0.58 & 63\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe complementary coverage hypothesis does not hold.\nSignal correlations range from 0.54 to 0.61, and 42\\% of flagged items are flagged by all three signals simultaneously, while only 15\\% are uniquely flagged by a single signal.\nThe three signals exhibit correlated failure modes---they all succeed on easy cases and all fail on partial or medium-effect contamination.\nWeighted fusion achieves F1\\,=\\,0.35, a modest gain over the best individual signal (ConStat, F1\\,=\\,0.32).\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 6: Cross-Benchmark Validation (GSM8K)}\n\\label{sec:exp6}\n\nWe replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items) using GSM1K as contamination ground truth.\nOnly 48 models have both GSM8K and GSM1K results.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K cross-validation results.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.30 & --- & --- \\\\\nDIF FPR & 0.13 & $\\leq 0.05$ & FAIL \\\\\nGSM8K--GSM1K gap correlation & 0.11 & --- & Negligible \\\\\n$N$ models & 48 & $\\geq 50$ & Insufficient \\\\\nIRT fit RMSEA & 0.09 & $\\leq 0.06$ & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe cross-benchmark result is negative.\nDIF performance on GSM8K mirrors MMLU: low TPR, high FPR, and near-zero correlation with the GSM1K ground truth ($\\rho = 0.11$).\nIRT model fit also fails (RMSEA\\,=\\,0.09), though less severely than MMLU due to smaller and more homogeneous model population.\nThe sample size (48 models) is below the minimum required for reliable DIF estimation.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 7: Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:exp7}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model to the MMLU response matrix and attempt test equating using anchor items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT model fit and equating results.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nConvergence rate & 73.5\\% & $\\geq 95\\%$ & FAIL \\\\\nGeneral factor variance & 0.35 & $\\geq 0.50$ & WEAK \\\\\nAnchor items available & 380 & $\\geq 500$ & MARGINAL \\\\\nEquating $\\rho$ & 0.85 & $\\geq 0.95$ & FAIL \\\\\nRank displacement (p95) & 12 & $\\leq 5$ & FAIL \\\\\nScore reliability (post) & 0.89 & $\\geq 0.92$ & MARGINAL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Factor structure summary.}\n\\label{tab:factors}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nFactor Type & $N$ Factors & Mean Loading & \\% Items $> 0.3$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nGeneral & 1 & 0.42 & 62\\% \\\\\nSpecific (STEM) & 14 & 0.31 & 38\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Humanities) & 18 & 0.26 & 29\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Social Sci.) & 15 & 0.28 & 33\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Other) & 10 & 0.24 & 25\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model only partially converges: 3,308 of 12,508 items (26.5\\%) fail to produce valid parameter estimates.\nThe general factor explains only 35\\% of response variance, indicating that MMLU is highly multidimensional---it does not primarily measure a single ``general LLM ability.''\nEquating using 380 anchor items improves ranking correlation from 0.82 to 0.85, but this falls well short of the psychometric equivalence standard ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$).\nThe 95th-percentile rank displacement of 12 positions means that models can shift substantially in rankings due to equating artifacts.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 8: Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:exp8}\n\nWe generate 150 replacement items and calibrate difficulty via student simulation and prompt-based targeting (180 GPU hours).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item calibration comparison.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Diff.\\ $\\rho$ & Diff.\\ MAE & Discrim.\\ $\\rho$ & Construct Align. \\\\\n\\midrule\nStudent simulation & 0.48 & 0.85 & 0.31 & 72\\% \\\\\nPrompt-based & 0.22 & 1.45 & 0.18 & 65\\% \\\\\nRandom baseline & 0.05 & 1.80 & 0.08 & 45\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Holdout validation on 30 models.}\n\\label{tab:holdout}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Student Sim. & Prompt-Based \\\\\n\\midrule\nDifficulty rank correlation & 0.44 & 0.19 \\\\\nScore equivalence $\\rho$ & 0.78 & 0.68 \\\\\nRanking preservation (top-10) & 70\\% & 50\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation outperforms prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.48$ vs.\\ 0.22) but absolute calibration quality is insufficient for reliable item replacement.\nDifficulty MAE of 0.85 logits means generated items deviate by nearly one standard deviation from their targets.\nDiscrimination calibration is poor ($\\rho = 0.31$), and 28\\% of items fail construct alignment---they measure different cognitive processes than the items they replace.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 9: Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:exp9}\n\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian priors to accumulate detection power across successive administrations.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power accumulation.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n$N$ Administrations & Power ($\\delta = 1.0$) & Power ($\\delta = 1.4$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.15 & 0.35 & 0.10 \\\\\n2 & 0.22 & 0.42 & 0.09 \\\\\n3 & 0.30 & 0.49 & 0.09 \\\\\n4 & 0.38 & 0.55 & 0.08 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBayesian accumulation yields marginal gains.\nAfter 4 administrations, power at $\\delta = 1.0$ reaches only 0.38 (theoretical: 0.70).\nThe prior shrinkage factor is 0.45 (ideal: $< 0.2$), indicating that model population heterogeneity severely limits prior transfer across administrations.\nFPR improves modestly from 0.10 to 0.08.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 10: Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{sec:exp10}\n\nWe quantify validity degradation under progressive item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement \\% & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability & Construct Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.00 & 0.94 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.94 & 0.92 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.88 & 0.88 & 0.93 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.79 & 0.81 & 0.85 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.62 & 0.67 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Critical replacement thresholds.}\n\\label{tab:thresholds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nValidity Criterion & Threshold & Max Safe Replacement \\\\\n\\midrule\nRanking $\\rho \\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 12\\% \\\\\nReliability $\\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 7\\% \\\\\nConstruct coverage $\\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 14\\% \\\\\nAll criteria met & --- & \\textbf{7\\%} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nUnder current calibration quality, at most 7\\% of MMLU items ($\\approx$875 items) can be safely replaced while maintaining all validity criteria.\nThis is far less than the number of potentially contaminated items.\nValidity degrades faster than expected because imperfect item calibration ($\\rho = 0.48$) and incomplete equating ($\\rho = 0.85$) both introduce systematic error that accumulates with replacement volume.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Supplementary: Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs an alternative to categorical cohort DIF, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) treating model release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ categorical DIF.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Categorical DIF & IFT & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.38 & 0.33 & $-$0.05 \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & 0.18 & 0.16 & $-$0.02 \\\\\nFPR & 0.12 & 0.10 & $-$0.02 \\\\\nF1 & 0.25 & 0.24 & $-$0.01 \\\\\nComputation (hours) & 0.5 & 18.0 & $+$17.5 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT does not improve detection.\nThe learned split points (concentrated in 2023-06 to 2024-01) align with architecture transitions rather than contamination events.\nCross-validation reveals a 0.12 TPR drop, indicating overfitting.\nThe 36$\\times$ computational cost yields no performance benefit.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Why the DIF Hypothesis Fails}\n\nThe fundamental issue is that DIF requires a well-fitting measurement model.\nThe 5,055 models in METABENCH span radically different architectures (encoder-only, decoder-only, MoE) whose response patterns are driven by architectural properties, not a single latent ability.\nRMSEA\\,=\\,0.11 and CFI\\,=\\,0.78 indicate severe model misfit.\nUnder these conditions, DIF conflates architecture-driven response differences with contamination-driven differences, producing an FPR of 0.12 that generates approximately 1,500 false positives on MMLU.\n\n\\subsection{The RL-Robustness Argument Collapses}\n\nThe theoretical argument that DIF is RL-robust rested on the assumption that RL shifts ICC difficulty without changing ICC shape.\nEmpirically, RL training (GRPO/PPO) alters both difficulty \\emph{and} discrimination parameters, as reward shaping changes response patterns across difficulty levels.\nDIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54 under RL, worse than ConStat (0.61), with a power retention ratio of 0.69---comparable to traditional methods rather than superior.\n\n\\subsection{Correlated Failure Modes}\n\nThe ensemble assumption of complementary coverage fails because the three signals share failure modes.\nCorrelations of 0.54--0.61 and 42\\% three-way overlap indicate that the signals largely flag the same (easy-to-detect) items while all missing hard cases.\nPartial memorization---the dominant real-world contamination mode---is difficult for all three methods.\n\n\\subsection{Practical Bounds on Regeneration}\n\nEven if detection were perfect, regeneration faces a hard constraint: current calibration quality limits safe replacement to 7\\% of items.\nThis creates a ``ceiling paradox'': if more than 7\\% of items are contaminated, the benchmark cannot self-heal without validity loss.\nThe bifactor analysis reveals that MMLU is highly multidimensional (general factor: 35\\% variance), suggesting that single-construct item replacement is ill-defined for many items.\n\n\\subsection{What Would Need to Change}\n\nFor DIF-based monitoring to become viable, we identify three necessary conditions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Architecture-stratified IRT}: Separate IRT models per architecture family (or a multi-group IRT framework) to eliminate architecture-driven spurious DIF.\n    \\item \\textbf{Larger RL cohorts}: The current 12 RL-trained models are insufficient. As the model ecosystem produces more RL-trained models, this constraint may resolve naturally.\n    \\item \\textbf{Better item calibration}: Difficulty calibration $\\rho$ must exceed 0.80 (currently 0.48) for replacement to be practical at the required scale.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe tested the hypothesis that DIF analysis can serve as an RL-robust benchmark contamination signal and found it does not hold on real data.\nThe heterogeneous LLM population violates the IRT assumptions DIF requires, producing false-positive rates that overwhelm contamination signals.\nThe RL-robustness advantage is absent, the multi-signal ensemble exhibits correlated rather than complementary failure modes, and regeneration is limited to at most 7\\% of items.\n\nThese negative results carry positive implications: they establish that naive application of classical psychometric methods to LLM evaluation requires careful attention to population heterogeneity, they provide the first large-scale characterization of IRT model fit on 5,000+ models, and they define practical bounds on benchmark self-repair.\nThe path forward likely requires architecture-aware IRT models---a direction we leave to future work.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2533,"cache_read_input_tokens":37294,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2533},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e9524a6-c1ea-4d71-a0f5-7ae7328ccf37","uuid":"659e46f5-37e4-47aa-89f4-373b998a8442"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JMQPp2abCMpPjeZtsiQmjD","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/worst/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e9524a6-c1ea-4d71-a0f5-7ae7328ccf37","uuid":"58f11a45-d277-471b-9972-90b7227f132f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:11:00.105Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\n\\title{When Psychometric Contamination Detection Meets Heterogeneous Model Populations:\\\\A Negative-Result Study on DIF-Based Benchmark Monitoring}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nBenchmark contamination threatens the validity of LLM evaluation, yet existing detection methods degrade under reinforcement learning (RL) training.\nWe investigate whether Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric technique---can serve as an RL-robust contamination signal by operating on group-level response patterns rather than within-model output distributions.\nWe test this hypothesis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and cross-validate on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth.\nOur findings are predominantly negative.\nThe unidimensional IRT model required for DIF severely misfits the heterogeneous model population (RMSEA\\,=\\,0.11, CFI\\,=\\,0.78), and architecture differences (encoder-only vs.\\ decoder-only vs.\\ MoE) generate massive spurious DIF that inflates the false-positive rate to 0.12.\nThe claimed RL-robustness advantage is not observed: DIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54 under RL cohorts, comparable to or worse than traditional detectors (ConStat AUROC\\,=\\,0.61).\nA three-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat + DVD) fails to provide complementary coverage due to correlated failure modes ($r$\\,=\\,0.54--0.61).\nBifactor IRT equating only partially converges (73.5\\% of items) and equated rankings reach $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.85, far below psychometric equivalence standards ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$).\nWe characterize the failure modes, identify the practical bounds of item replacement (at most 7\\% of items can be safely replaced), and argue that DIF-based benchmark monitoring requires architecture-stratified modeling before it can be viable.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM benchmarks such as MMLU implicitly assume temporal score comparability: when we say ``Model B outperforms Model A,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent ability rather than differential exposure to test content.\nBenchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption.\nThe problem is compounded by the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1), as Wang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) demonstrate 14--20pp AUROC degradation for reference-free detectors after approximately 156 GRPO steps.\n\nWe hypothesized that Differential Item Functioning (DIF)---a technique from educational testing that detects items behaving differently across examinee groups after controlling for ability---could provide an RL-robust contamination signal.\nThe theoretical argument is that DIF operates on \\emph{group-level} Item Characteristic Curves (ICCs) conditional on ability, making it orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing caused by RL.\nWe further proposed a full pipeline: DIF-based detection, multi-signal ensemble fusion for full-spectrum coverage, and bifactor IRT equating for calibrated item replacement.\n\nThis paper reports the results of this investigation across 10 experiments.\nThe core finding is negative: \\textbf{the DIF-based contamination detection hypothesis does not hold on real data} because the 5,000+ heterogeneous model population massively violates the IRT unidimensionality assumption that DIF requires.\nArchitecture heterogeneity creates spurious DIF that overwhelms the contamination signal.\n\nDespite the negative headline result, we make several empirical contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item We provide the first large-scale empirical characterization of IRT model fit on heterogeneous LLM populations, documenting systematic failure modes.\n    \\item We establish practical bounds on benchmark item replacement under imperfect calibration (at most 7\\% safe replacement).\n    \\item We demonstrate that three contamination detection signals (DIF, ConStat, DVD) exhibit correlated failure modes rather than complementary coverage, challenging the ensemble assumption.\n    \\item We show that bifactor IRT reveals MMLU to be highly multidimensional (general factor explains only 35\\% of variance), with implications for benchmark design.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMin-K\\% Prob identifies contamination through low-perplexity tokens.\nConStat provides FDR-controlled detection via perturbation sensitivity.\nDVD detects variant contamination through generation variance.\nAll these methods operate on within-model output distributions and are vulnerable to RL smoothing.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}\nDIF analysis identifies test items that function differently across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability.\nThe ETS classification system uses both statistical significance and effect-size thresholds ($\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$) to flag items.\nDIF has been applied extensively in human testing but, to our knowledge, never to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}\nGrowing Pains (2026) applies multidimensional IRT to LLM evaluation for extensible benchmark design.\nOur work differs in targeting contamination detection rather than score comparability, though our bifactor analysis yields related insights about benchmark dimensionality.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark regeneration.}\nLiveBench updates monthly from recent sources without psychometric calibration.\nStudent simulation (arXiv 2601.09953) uses capability-ablated LLMs for difficulty estimation.\nNo prior work integrates detection, equating, and regeneration into a closed loop.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\nWe partition models into temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024, or annual/sliding-window cohorts) and fit a unidimensional 2PL IRT model to the binary response matrix.\nFor each item $j$, we compute the Mantel--Haenszel DIF statistic $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},j}$ between the reference (earlier) and focal (later) cohorts.\nItems are classified using the ETS C criterion ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant at $\\alpha = 0.05$).\nA contaminated item should exhibit DIF because exposed models perform better than their overall ability predicts.\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\nWe combine three contamination signals:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF} (ETS C): group-level ICC shift, hypothesized RL-robust.\n    \\item \\textbf{ConStat}: perturbation sensitivity---contaminated models answer originals correctly but perturbations incorrectly.\n    \\item \\textbf{DVD}: generation variance---contaminated items elicit bimodal response distributions.\n\\end{itemize}\nFusion strategies include majority voting (2-of-3) and weighted fusion with weights learned on a validation split.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating and Regeneration}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model with one general factor and subject-specific factors (57 MMLU subjects).\nAnchor items (stable across cohorts, no DIF) enable score equating.\nFlagged items are replaced by LLM-generated alternatives calibrated via student simulation.\nA graceful-degradation framework quantifies validity loss under progressive item replacement.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) unless otherwise noted.\nWe use MMLU-CF as partial ground truth for MMLU and GSM1K for GSM8K.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 1: DIF Detection on Real Data (MMLU)}\n\\label{sec:exp1}\n\nWe apply temporal cohort DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix using three cohort definitions: binary (pre/post-2024), annual, and 6-month sliding window.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_performance} reports detection performance.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection performance on METABENCH MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:dif_performance}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Pre/Post-2024 & Annual & Sliding 6mo \\\\\n\\midrule\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.38 & 0.31 & 0.28 \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & 0.18 & 0.14 & 0.12 \\\\\nFPR & 0.12 & 0.14 & 0.15 \\\\\nF1 ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.33 & 0.28 & 0.25 \\\\\nItems flagged & 1,840 & 2,100 & 2,350 \\\\\nMMLU-CF $\\phi$ correlation & 0.09 & 0.07 & 0.06 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe IRT model severely misfits the data.\nTable~\\ref{tab:irt_fit} shows that all fit indices fall outside acceptable thresholds.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IRT model fit diagnostics.}\n\\label{tab:irt_fit}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nFit Index & Value & Threshold & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.11 & $\\leq 0.06$ & FAIL \\\\\nCFI & 0.78 & $\\geq 0.90$ & FAIL \\\\\nTLI & 0.74 & $\\geq 0.90$ & FAIL \\\\\nSRMR & 0.09 & $\\leq 0.08$ & FAIL \\\\\n$M^2$ $p$-value & $<$0.001 & $>$0.05 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe unidimensionality assumption is rejected ($p < 0.001$).\nItem discrimination variance is 2.8, far exceeding typical values ($<1.0$), indicating that architecture differences---not contamination---dominate the response patterns.\nThe FPR of 0.12 means approximately 1,500 false positives among the 12,508 items, overwhelming any true contamination signal.\nCorrelation with MMLU-CF ground truth is near zero ($\\phi = 0.09$).\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 2: RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:exp2}\n\nWe compare DIF and traditional detectors on SFT-only vs.\\ RL-inclusive cohorts.\nOnly 12 RL-trained models are identifiable in METABENCH.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{RL-robustness comparison: DIF vs.\\ traditional detectors.}\n\\label{tab:rl_comparison}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Retention & $\\Delta$AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS C) & 0.67 & 0.54 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.74 & 0.58 & 0.78 & $-$0.16 \\\\\nConStat & 0.71 & 0.61 & 0.86 & $-$0.10 \\\\\nDVD & 0.68 & 0.55 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\nPerplexity & 0.69 & 0.56 & 0.81 & $-$0.13 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF TPR under SFT-only vs.\\ RL-inclusive cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:dif_tpr_rl}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size & TPR (SFT) & TPR (RL) & Retention \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.6$ & 0.48 & 0.35 & 0.73 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.42 & 0.29 & 0.69 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.0$ & 0.20 & 0.13 & 0.65 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.6$ & 0.09 & 0.06 & 0.67 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF does not exhibit the hypothesized RL-robustness.\nUnder RL cohorts, DIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54, which is \\emph{worse} than ConStat (0.61).\nThe AUROC gap between DIF and the best traditional method under RL is only 0.02 in DIF's \\emph{disfavor}.\nPower retention ratio is 0.69, comparable to traditional methods (0.78--0.86).\nThe theoretical argument that ``DIF operates on ICC shape, which RL does not alter'' fails because RL training (GRPO/PPO) changes ICC shape itself, not just difficulty.\nAdditionally, the RL cohort contains only 12 models, limiting statistical reliability.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 3: ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp3}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing independently on MMLU with 220 generated perturbation items (82\\% semantic equivalence rate, mean similarity 0.91).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection by effect size and model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size & TPR (SFT) & TPR (RL) & TPR (Mixed) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.55 & 0.18 & 0.38 & 0.06 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 1.0$ & 0.45 & 0.14 & 0.30 & 0.06 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.6$ & 0.32 & 0.11 & 0.22 & 0.07 \\\\\n$\\delta \\geq 0.4$ & 0.19 & 0.08 & 0.14 & 0.07 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat shows moderate power on SFT models for large effects (TPR\\,=\\,0.55 at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$) but degrades sharply under RL (TPR\\,=\\,0.18).\nFDR-controlled power is only 0.28.\nReal-world contamination is predominantly partial memorization; models that only remember answer patterns rather than exact wording are insensitive to perturbations.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 4: DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp4}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 500 sampled items across 13 models with 50 generations per item (65 GPU hours).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nType & TPR & FPR & AUROC & $N$ items \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 0.48 & 0.09 & 0.72 & 120 \\\\\nVariant & 0.22 & 0.09 & 0.58 & 180 \\\\\nPartial & 0.12 & 0.09 & 0.52 & 200 \\\\\nOverall & 0.25 & 0.09 & 0.61 & 500 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD's bimodal-variance assumption breaks down for partial contamination (TPR\\,=\\,0.12), which is the dominant real-world contamination mode.\nThe bimodal distribution rate is only 18\\% for contaminated items vs.\\ 7\\% for clean items---statistically significant but with an effect size too small for reliable per-item detection.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 5: Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:exp5}\n\nWe combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual signal performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR$_{\\geq 1.4}$ & TPR$_{\\geq 1.0}$ & TPR$_{\\geq 0.6}$ & FPR & F1 \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.38 & 0.18 & 0.09 & 0.12 & 0.25 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.38 & 0.30 & 0.22 & 0.06 & 0.32 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.25 & 0.15 & 0.10 & 0.09 & 0.18 \\\\\nMajority (2/3) & 0.38 & 0.19 & 0.11 & 0.05 & 0.30 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & 0.52 & 0.30 & 0.18 & 0.11 & 0.35 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Signal correlation matrix.}\n\\label{tab:correlation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nSignal Pair & Pearson $r$ & Flagged-Item Overlap \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF--ConStat & 0.61 & 68\\% \\\\\nDIF--DVD & 0.54 & 59\\% \\\\\nConStat--DVD & 0.58 & 63\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe complementary coverage hypothesis does not hold.\nSignal correlations range from 0.54 to 0.61, and 42\\% of flagged items are flagged by all three signals simultaneously, while only 15\\% are uniquely flagged by a single signal.\nThe three signals exhibit correlated failure modes---they all succeed on easy cases and all fail on partial or medium-effect contamination.\nWeighted fusion achieves F1\\,=\\,0.35, a modest gain over the best individual signal (ConStat, F1\\,=\\,0.32).\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 6: Cross-Benchmark Validation (GSM8K)}\n\\label{sec:exp6}\n\nWe replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items) using GSM1K as contamination ground truth.\nOnly 48 models have both GSM8K and GSM1K results.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K cross-validation results.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.30 & --- & --- \\\\\nDIF FPR & 0.13 & $\\leq 0.05$ & FAIL \\\\\nGSM8K--GSM1K gap correlation & 0.11 & --- & Negligible \\\\\n$N$ models & 48 & $\\geq 50$ & Insufficient \\\\\nIRT fit RMSEA & 0.09 & $\\leq 0.06$ & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe cross-benchmark result is negative.\nDIF performance on GSM8K mirrors MMLU: low TPR, high FPR, and near-zero correlation with the GSM1K ground truth ($\\rho = 0.11$).\nIRT model fit also fails (RMSEA\\,=\\,0.09), though less severely than MMLU due to smaller and more homogeneous model population.\nThe sample size (48 models) is below the minimum required for reliable DIF estimation.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 7: Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:exp7}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model to the MMLU response matrix and attempt test equating using anchor items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT model fit and equating results.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nConvergence rate & 73.5\\% & $\\geq 95\\%$ & FAIL \\\\\nGeneral factor variance & 0.35 & $\\geq 0.50$ & WEAK \\\\\nAnchor items available & 380 & $\\geq 500$ & MARGINAL \\\\\nEquating $\\rho$ & 0.85 & $\\geq 0.95$ & FAIL \\\\\nRank displacement (p95) & 12 & $\\leq 5$ & FAIL \\\\\nScore reliability (post) & 0.89 & $\\geq 0.92$ & MARGINAL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Factor structure summary.}\n\\label{tab:factors}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nFactor Type & $N$ Factors & Mean Loading & \\% Items $> 0.3$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nGeneral & 1 & 0.42 & 62\\% \\\\\nSpecific (STEM) & 14 & 0.31 & 38\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Humanities) & 18 & 0.26 & 29\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Social Sci.) & 15 & 0.28 & 33\\% \\\\\nSpecific (Other) & 10 & 0.24 & 25\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model only partially converges: 3,308 of 12,508 items (26.5\\%) fail to produce valid parameter estimates.\nThe general factor explains only 35\\% of response variance, indicating that MMLU is highly multidimensional---it does not primarily measure a single ``general LLM ability.''\nEquating using 380 anchor items improves ranking correlation from 0.82 to 0.85, but this falls well short of the psychometric equivalence standard ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$).\nThe 95th-percentile rank displacement of 12 positions means that models can shift substantially in rankings due to equating artifacts.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 8: Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:exp8}\n\nWe generate 150 replacement items and calibrate difficulty via student simulation and prompt-based targeting (180 GPU hours).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item calibration comparison.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Diff.\\ $\\rho$ & Diff.\\ MAE & Discrim.\\ $\\rho$ & Construct Align. \\\\\n\\midrule\nStudent simulation & 0.48 & 0.85 & 0.31 & 72\\% \\\\\nPrompt-based & 0.22 & 1.45 & 0.18 & 65\\% \\\\\nRandom baseline & 0.05 & 1.80 & 0.08 & 45\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Holdout validation on 30 models.}\n\\label{tab:holdout}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Student Sim. & Prompt-Based \\\\\n\\midrule\nDifficulty rank correlation & 0.44 & 0.19 \\\\\nScore equivalence $\\rho$ & 0.78 & 0.68 \\\\\nRanking preservation (top-10) & 70\\% & 50\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation outperforms prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.48$ vs.\\ 0.22) but absolute calibration quality is insufficient for reliable item replacement.\nDifficulty MAE of 0.85 logits means generated items deviate by nearly one standard deviation from their targets.\nDiscrimination calibration is poor ($\\rho = 0.31$), and 28\\% of items fail construct alignment---they measure different cognitive processes than the items they replace.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 9: Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:exp9}\n\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian priors to accumulate detection power across successive administrations.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power accumulation.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n$N$ Administrations & Power ($\\delta = 1.0$) & Power ($\\delta = 1.4$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.15 & 0.35 & 0.10 \\\\\n2 & 0.22 & 0.42 & 0.09 \\\\\n3 & 0.30 & 0.49 & 0.09 \\\\\n4 & 0.38 & 0.55 & 0.08 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBayesian accumulation yields marginal gains.\nAfter 4 administrations, power at $\\delta = 1.0$ reaches only 0.38 (theoretical: 0.70).\nThe prior shrinkage factor is 0.45 (ideal: $< 0.2$), indicating that model population heterogeneity severely limits prior transfer across administrations.\nFPR improves modestly from 0.10 to 0.08.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Experiment 10: Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{sec:exp10}\n\nWe quantify validity degradation under progressive item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement \\% & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability & Construct Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.00 & 0.94 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.94 & 0.92 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.88 & 0.88 & 0.93 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.79 & 0.81 & 0.85 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.62 & 0.67 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Critical replacement thresholds.}\n\\label{tab:thresholds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nValidity Criterion & Threshold & Max Safe Replacement \\\\\n\\midrule\nRanking $\\rho \\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 12\\% \\\\\nReliability $\\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 7\\% \\\\\nConstruct coverage $\\geq 0.90$ & 0.90 & 14\\% \\\\\nAll criteria met & --- & \\textbf{7\\%} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nUnder current calibration quality, at most 7\\% of MMLU items ($\\approx$875 items) can be safely replaced while maintaining all validity criteria.\nThis is far less than the number of potentially contaminated items.\nValidity degrades faster than expected because imperfect item calibration ($\\rho = 0.48$) and incomplete equating ($\\rho = 0.85$) both introduce systematic error that accumulates with replacement volume.\n\n% ------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Supplementary: Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs an alternative to categorical cohort DIF, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) treating model release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ categorical DIF.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Categorical DIF & IFT & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.38 & 0.33 & $-$0.05 \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & 0.18 & 0.16 & $-$0.02 \\\\\nFPR & 0.12 & 0.10 & $-$0.02 \\\\\nF1 & 0.25 & 0.24 & $-$0.01 \\\\\nComputation (hours) & 0.5 & 18.0 & $+$17.5 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT does not improve detection.\nThe learned split points (concentrated in 2023-06 to 2024-01) align with architecture transitions rather than contamination events.\nCross-validation reveals a 0.12 TPR drop, indicating overfitting.\nThe 36$\\times$ computational cost yields no performance benefit.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Why the DIF Hypothesis Fails}\n\nThe fundamental issue is that DIF requires a well-fitting measurement model.\nThe 5,055 models in METABENCH span radically different architectures (encoder-only, decoder-only, MoE) whose response patterns are driven by architectural properties, not a single latent ability.\nRMSEA\\,=\\,0.11 and CFI\\,=\\,0.78 indicate severe model misfit.\nUnder these conditions, DIF conflates architecture-driven response differences with contamination-driven differences, producing an FPR of 0.12 that generates approximately 1,500 false positives on MMLU.\n\n\\subsection{The RL-Robustness Argument Collapses}\n\nThe theoretical argument that DIF is RL-robust rested on the assumption that RL shifts ICC difficulty without changing ICC shape.\nEmpirically, RL training (GRPO/PPO) alters both difficulty \\emph{and} discrimination parameters, as reward shaping changes response patterns across difficulty levels.\nDIF achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.54 under RL, worse than ConStat (0.61), with a power retention ratio of 0.69---comparable to traditional methods rather than superior.\n\n\\subsection{Correlated Failure Modes}\n\nThe ensemble assumption of complementary coverage fails because the three signals share failure modes.\nCorrelations of 0.54--0.61 and 42\\% three-way overlap indicate that the signals largely flag the same (easy-to-detect) items while all missing hard cases.\nPartial memorization---the dominant real-world contamination mode---is difficult for all three methods.\n\n\\subsection{Practical Bounds on Regeneration}\n\nEven if detection were perfect, regeneration faces a hard constraint: current calibration quality limits safe replacement to 7\\% of items.\nThis creates a ``ceiling paradox'': if more than 7\\% of items are contaminated, the benchmark cannot self-heal without validity loss.\nThe bifactor analysis reveals that MMLU is highly multidimensional (general factor: 35\\% variance), suggesting that single-construct item replacement is ill-defined for many items.\n\n\\subsection{What Would Need to Change}\n\nFor DIF-based monitoring to become viable, we identify three necessary conditions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Architecture-stratified IRT}: Separate IRT models per architecture family (or a multi-group IRT framework) to eliminate architecture-driven spurious DIF.\n    \\item \\textbf{Larger RL cohorts}: The current 12 RL-trained models are insufficient. As the model ecosystem produces more RL-trained models, this constraint may resolve naturally.\n    \\item \\textbf{Better item calibration}: Difficulty calibration $\\rho$ must exceed 0.80 (currently 0.48) for replacement to be practical at the required scale.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe tested the hypothesis that DIF analysis can serve as an RL-robust benchmark contamination signal and found it does not hold on real data.\nThe heterogeneous LLM population violates the IRT assumptions DIF requires, producing false-positive rates that overwhelm contamination signals.\nThe RL-robustness advantage is absent, the multi-signal ensemble exhibits correlated rather than complementary failure modes, and regeneration is limited to at most 7\\% of items.\n\nThese negative results carry positive implications: they establish that naive application of classical psychometric methods to LLM evaluation requires careful attention to population heterogeneity, they provide the first large-scale characterization of IRT model fit on 5,000+ models, and they define practical bounds on benchmark self-repair.\nThe path forward likely requires architecture-aware IRT models---a direction we leave to future work.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
